{
  "abstract": "Cubic excess-two Lehman pairs are binary matrices $B,L$ satisfying $BL^{\\mathsf T}=J+2I$, with $B$ having all row and column sums three. Such pairs supply directed strongly regular graphs through a cubic flag construction. We prove that no pair of order $34$ exists, without imposing symmetry or a prescribed incidence pattern. The binary kernel of $B$ has exactly $34$ words of weight $22$, and its dual has exactly $34$ words of weight three and none of weights one or two. A cubic Krawtchouk moment identity then forces a nonzero kernel word of weight at most twelve. Elementary incidence arguments exclude weights four and six. For the remaining weights, two complete local mate enumerations reduce the possibilities to four support interfaces. Matching-type population equations, solved by exact elimination and weak-composition enumeration, give $81$ complete incidence cases. Every Boolean completion is refuted with a proof checked by DRAT-trim. The supplied code regenerates the cover, formulas and checked proofs and reconstructs known order-$28$ controls. The result excludes the cubic excess-two ingredient for the flag construction of a $(102,37,19,14,13)$ directed strongly regular graph. It does not establish nonexistence of arbitrary graphs with those parameters. The short-support forcing argument and finite interface description provide a reusable approach to constrained binary matrix factorizations.",
